vulkan: the Vulkan renderer runs in the sandbox, on Chimera's Vulkan

xemu's Vulkan renderer (hw/xbox/nv2a/pgraph/vk) is built into the sandboxed
core and runs on the Vulkan device Chimera hands a core through miniBox's
Vulkan bridge (chimera issue 248). The driver picks it when the Renderer
setting is 'vulkan-hw'.

The build. A cross build declares Vulkan itself (meson.build): the headers
are miniBox's, behind a vulkan/vulkan.h that is the two of them
(pgraph/vk/chimera/vulkan/vulkan.h), and the commands are the guest half of
the bridge, generated from miniBox's list (pgraph/vk/meson.build; the option
chimera_minibox says where miniBox is). glslang, volk, SPIRV-Reflect and the
Vulkan Memory Allocator are built as upstream builds them. volk is kept from
looking for a Vulkan of its own, which found the build machine's headers and
put /usr/include in front of the sandbox's C library.

No window, and no OpenGL to share the frame with (HAVE_EXTERNAL_MEMORY 0).
The frame the display code composes is copied to a buffer and read back
(chimera.c, nv2a_chimera_vk_read_display), the right way up and with its
fourth byte as the game left it, as the OpenGL renderer's read gives it.

No pfifo thread. Every wait for it does the pending work itself first
(pfifo_service_pending), as the OpenGL renderer's waits do since patch 0009.
Without that the machine stopped with every thread waiting.

Mapped memory. Through the bridge no memory is coherent and a mapping is a
copy in the machine's own memory, so:
- the probe of the GPU's properties asks for memory that is only host
  visible. It asked for coherent memory, found no such type, and passed
  "none" (-1) to the driver as the type's number, which ended the frontend
  inside a GeForce's driver;
- the staged index, vertex and uniform data is flushed before it is copied
  to the device, and every flush or invalidate names the bytes that matter
  where upstream names the whole buffer (CHIMERA_VK_BYTES);
- the buffers that stay mapped are smaller: 8 MiB of indices and 32 MiB of
  inline vertices where upstream has 200 MiB and 1.3 GiB, 32 MiB for each
  staging buffer where it has 64. A draw too large for them stops the
  machine with an assertion;
- the copy of the console's RAM that vertices are drawn from is not filled
  when the renderer starts. All of RAM is marked as changed instead, and a
  range is copied the first time a draw needs it.

A load of the machine is a new Vulkan device. The renderer asks which device
it is on where it is about to use one (pgraph_vk_check_context) and, when it
has changed, lets go of everything with the bridge silent and starts again,
as upstream does when the user changes renderer. What the GPU drew is written
into the console's RAM before a state is taken (nv2a_chimera_vk_flush_surfaces,
the twin of patch 0019), so the renderer made again finds it there.

Being made again quickly. That start-up ran once on a desktop and runs after
every load here: 1.0 s a load on a GeForce GTX 1060, against 0.3 s for the
OpenGL renderer. It is about 0.35 s with: each shader's SPIR-V kept by its
text for as long as the machine runs (glsl.c), the shader compiler started
once and not stopped, a shader module table of four thousand entries where
upstream has fifty thousand, depth-conversion buffers of 300 MiB where it
has 840, and the RAM copy above.

